# Substance 3D Designer filter nodes, documentation only

- Date: 11 October 2026
- Scope of this pass: Filters > Adjustments, Filters > Blending, Filters > Blurs, Filters > Effects (warp and distance nodes only), Filters > Transforms, and the atomic nodes Blend, Blur, Directional blur, Directional warp, Warp, Curve, Levels, Distance and Transformation 2D.
- Not in this pass: the other Filters > Effects nodes (Bevel, Glow, Edge Detect, Emboss, Shadows and others), Filters > Channels, Filters > Normal Map, Filters > Tiling, Material filters, texture generators, mesh-based generators, 3D view nodes, function graph nodes.
- Method: Substance 3D Designer is not installed on this machine. Every node and page below was read with WebSearch and WebFetch on Adobe's official Substance 3D Designer documentation (Experience League). The node list comes from the official node library index: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/node-library (read 11 October 2026). The HelpX copies of the same pages returned HTTP 403 to WebFetch, so the Experience League pages were used.
- Names: node titles are written exactly as the official pages title them. Grayscale variants named on a page are listed inside their parent section and are not counted as separate nodes.
- Copernicus names: taken only from /Users/zenray/.claude/skills/zenray-cop-designer/inventory/cop_names.txt. The "Cop" family is the Copernicus context. The "Cop2" family is the legacy COP2 context and is still listed in cop_names.txt. A Cop2 name is used only when no Cop-family name is close, and it is flagged "legacy COP2".
- Fit levels: close (same operation), approximate (similar operation, different parameters or behaviour), none (no equivalent in cop_names.txt).
- Mapping status: every mapping on this page is documented_only. No mapping was tested in Houdini.
- Defaults and numeric ranges: written "not stated" wherever the official page does not give them. No value was guessed.
- Blend modes: the Blend node takes 12 blend modes. They are listed once, inside section 1, and are not counted as nodes.

